To center an image (or any child element) both vertically and horizontally within a container using only parent-level CSS, you can apply either Flexbox or CSS Grid. Here are the two recommended modern methods:

### Option 1: Flexbox (Most Common & Flexible)
```css
.style {
  width: 100vh;
  height: 100vh;
  display: flex;
  justify-content: center; /* Horizontal centering */
  align-items: center;     /* Vertical centering */
}
```

HTML:
```html
<div class="style">
    <img src="img/world.png" alt="World">
</div>
```

### Option 2: CSS Grid (Concise & Powerful)
```css
.style {
  width: 100vh;
  height: 100vh;
  display: grid;
  place-items: center; /* Centers both children in one line */
}
```

### ✅ Bonus Tips:
- Use `object-fit: contain` to prevent images from overflowing their space:
  ```css
  .style > img {
      max-width: 100%;
      height: auto;
      object-fit: contain;
  }
  ```

Both Flexbox and CSS Grid are fully supported in all major browsers and require no JavaScript. You can choose Flexbox for its simplicity across many contexts, or CSS Grid if you prefer concise syntax and advanced layout capabilities.

